.Dd June 1, 2026
.Dt XTC_TRACE 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_trace_enable ,
.Nm xtc_trace_reset ,
.Nm xtc_trace_dump ,
.Nm xtc_hlc_now ,
.Nm xtc_trace_causal_enable ,
.Nm xtc_trace_causal_dump
.Nd causal message tracing (libxtc's seq_trace) and the per-fiber async causal trace
.Sh LIBRARY
libxtc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_trace.h
.Vt typedef int (*xtc_trace_fn)(const xtc_trace_rec_t *rec, void *user);
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_trace_enable "int on"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_trace_reset "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_trace_dump "xtc_trace_fn cb" "void *user"
.Ft uint64_t
.Fn xtc_hlc_now "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_trace_causal_enable "int on"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_trace_causal_dump "xtc_pid_t pid" "xtc_causal_fn cb" "void *user"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_trace
is libxtc's
.Sy seq_trace :
a bounded, opt-in ring of message-passing events -- each send and
receive between procs -- stamped so that the causal order of one
request can be reconstructed across procs and cores even when the
wall-clock order in which the records arrived lies.
It is the analog of Erlang's trace token that rides along message
sends, letting you answer
.Dq who sent this, and what caused that
in a system where the answer is spread across many mailboxes.
.Pp
Every traced event is stamped with a hybrid logical clock
.Pq Sy HLC :
a 64-bit value whose high 48 bits are a monotonic physical-time
component (microseconds) and whose low 16 bits are a logical counter.
A single global clock ticks once per traced event, so a send's stamp
is always strictly less than the stamp of the receive it causes.
Sorting the records by HLC therefore yields a true happens-before
order, not a thread-local arrival order: when two cores disagree about
which message landed first, the HLC is the truth.
.Pp
The causal edge is made explicit, not merely implied by ordering: a
.Dv XTC_TRACE_RECV
record carries the originating send's HLC stamp in its
.Va cause
field.  Following
.Va cause
from a receive back to the matching send, and from that send's proc to
the receive that triggered it, walks the chain of one request as it
fans out across procs.
.Pp
Tracing is OFF by default.  When disabled it costs a single relaxed
atomic load on the message hot path, so a production build that never
enables it pays essentially nothing.  When enabled it serializes ring
writes under a lock and overwrites the oldest records once the ring is
full.  Enable it to debug a specific problem -- as you would reach for
.Sy recon
or
.Sy dbg
on the BEAM -- not as an always-on production tax.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_trace_enable
turns tracing on when
.Fa on
is non-zero and off when it is zero.  It returns the previous state
(non-zero if tracing was on), so a caller can save and restore it.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_trace_reset
drops all buffered trace records, leaving the enable state unchanged.
It returns
.Dv XTC_OK .
Call it before a workload to ensure the ring holds only the events of
interest.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_trace_dump
visits the buffered records in causal (HLC-ascending) order, invoking
.Fa cb
once per record with a pointer to the record and the caller's
.Fa user
pointer.  The callback returns
.Dv 0
to continue or any non-zero value to stop the walk early.  The
callbacks run
.Em after
the internal trace lock is released, so a callback may freely call
back into libxtc -- including
.Fn xtc_send ,
the inspection API, or
.Fn xtc_trace_dump
itself -- without risk of self-deadlock.  It returns the number of
records visited, or a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code on error.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_hlc_now
returns the current value of the global HLC.  It is intended for tests
and for display; an application does not normally need it, since the
stamps already ride in the trace records.
.Pp
A trace record is reported in:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct xtc_trace_rec {
        uint64_t  hlc;     /* this event's HLC stamp */
        uint64_t  cause;   /* RECV: the originating send's HLC; else 0 */
        int       kind;    /* enum xtc_trace_kind */
        xtc_pid_t self;    /* the proc the event happened in */
        xtc_pid_t peer;    /* the other proc (dest / source / child) */
        uint32_t  detail;  /* SEND/RECV: payload bytes; EXIT: reason */
} xtc_trace_rec_t;
.Ed
.Pp
The fields are:
.Bl -tag -width "cause" -offset indent
.It Va hlc
This event's HLC stamp.  Records sort by this value into causal order.
.It Va cause
For a
.Dv XTC_TRACE_RECV
record, the HLC stamp of the send that produced the received message;
.Dv 0
for every other kind.  This is the explicit causal edge.
.It Va kind
One of the
.Vt enum xtc_trace_kind
values below.
.It Va self
The proc in which the event happened: the sender for a send, the
receiver for a receive, the parent for a spawn, the exiting proc for an
exit.
.It Va peer
The other proc involved: the destination of a send, the source of a
receive, or the spawned child.
.It Va detail
For a send or receive, the message payload size in bytes; for an exit,
the exit reason.
.El
.Pp
.Vt enum xtc_trace_kind
names the event kinds:
.Bl -tag -width XTC_TRACE_SPAWN -compact -offset indent
.It Dv XTC_TRACE_SEND
.Va self
sent a message to
.Va peer .
.It Dv XTC_TRACE_RECV
.Va self
received a message from
.Va peer ;
.Va cause
holds the originating send's stamp.
.It Dv XTC_TRACE_SPAWN
.Va self
(the parent) spawned
.Va peer
(the child).
.It Dv XTC_TRACE_EXIT
.Va self
exited;
.Va detail
holds the exit reason.
.El
.Pp
The visit callback has the type
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef int (*xtc_trace_fn)(const xtc_trace_rec_t *rec, void *user);
.Ed
.Pp
where
.Fa rec
points at one record (valid only for the duration of the call) and
.Fa user
is the pointer passed to
.Fn xtc_trace_dump .
.Pp
The per-shard HLC used by the MVCC data path
is a separate, later use of the same clock idea; it is not the global
trace clock described here.
.Sh ASYNC CAUSAL TRACE
Where the message trace above records the causal chain of messages
.Em between
procs, the
.Sy async causal trace
records the suspend/resume chain
.Em within
one proc: the ordered call sites at which a fiber parked and resumed
.Pq a mailbox receive, a timer sleep, an fd wait .
It answers a different question --
.Dq how did THIS fiber get here
-- and complements the current-state view of
.Xr xtc_dump 3 ,
which reports what a proc's state is now but not the path of
suspensions that led to it.  It is the C analog of Cats Effect's
per-fiber async-stack-trace ring, which is spliced onto a fault or
fiber dump.
.Pp
Each proc carries a small fixed-capacity ring
.Pq 16 entries
of records:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct xtc_causal_rec {
        int          kind;    /* enum xtc_causal_kind */
        const char  *site;    /* static label (e.g. __func__) */
} xtc_causal_rec_t;
.Ed
where
.Va kind
is one of
.Dv XTC_CAUSAL_PARK_MAILBOX ,
.Dv XTC_CAUSAL_PARK_TIMER ,
.Dv XTC_CAUSAL_PARK_FD ,
or
.Dv XTC_CAUSAL_RESUME ,
and
.Va site
is a static string label (never freed).  Once the ring is full the
oldest record is overwritten, so the window holds the most recent 16
boundaries.
.Pp
The async causal trace is OFF by default and ZERO-COST when off: a
disabled trace is a single relaxed-atomic load and a branch at the
suspend/resume boundary and touches no memory, so a default build
records nothing.  Each recorded event is one index bump and a store on
the owning fiber -- core-private, single-writer, no lock, no atomic on
the record, and no allocation after spawn -- so a fiber never writes a
shared cache line on the hot path even when the trace is on.  The
recorder reads no clock and draws no randomness, so a run remains
deterministic under the simulation backend with the trace enabled.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_trace_causal_enable
turns the async causal trace on when
.Fa on
is non-zero and off when it is zero, returning the previous state
.Pq 1 on, 0 off .
This is a separate switch from
.Fn xtc_trace_enable ,
which governs the message trace above.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_trace_causal_dump
visits the causal records of the proc
.Fa pid
oldest-first -- the fiber's recent park/resume chain -- invoking
.Fa cb
once per record.  The callback returns
.Dv 0
to continue or non-zero to stop early.  It returns the number of
records visited,
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
if no live proc has that pid (a proc that has exited has been reaped),
or
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
on a
.Dv NULL
callback.  When the trace is disabled a live proc simply has an empty
ring.
.Pp
.Xr xtc_dump 3
splices each proc's recent causal chain onto its state line when the
async causal trace is enabled -- a
.Ql causal:
line of
.Ql kind@site
entries, oldest-first -- so a dump answers both
.Dq what is this fiber's state now
and
.Dq how did it get here
at once.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_trace_enable
returns the previous enable state: non-zero if tracing was already on,
.Dv 0
if it was off.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_trace_reset
returns
.Dv XTC_OK .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_trace_dump
returns the number of records visited, or a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code on error.  See
.Xr xtc_strerror 3 .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_hlc_now
returns the current global HLC value.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Enable tracing, run a workload, and print the records in causal order:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static int
on_rec(const xtc_trace_rec_t *r, void *user)
{
        (void)user;
        printf("HLC=%llu kind=%d self=%llu peer=%llu cause=%llu det=%u\\n",
            (unsigned long long)r->hlc, r->kind,
            (unsigned long long)r->self,
            (unsigned long long)r->peer,
            (unsigned long long)r->cause, r->detail);
        return (0);                 /* 0 = keep going */
}

int was_on = xtc_trace_enable(1);   /* opt in */
xtc_trace_reset();                  /* start from a clean ring */
run_workload();                     /* exercise the procs in question */

int n = xtc_trace_dump(on_rec, NULL);
printf("%d trace records\\n", n);

if (!was_on)
        xtc_trace_enable(0);        /* stop paying for it */
.Ed
.Pp
Reading the dump, a
.Dv XTC_TRACE_RECV
whose
.Va cause
equals the
.Va hlc
of an earlier
.Dv XTC_TRACE_SEND
is the same message; chaining those edges reconstructs a request's path
across procs.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_inspect 3 ,
.Xr xtc_proc 3 ,
.Xr xtc_stats 3
